#!/usr/bin/env -S agent run -w -e high -m opus
You exfiltrate a topic: you get the agent sessions that discussed it to write what they know
into Dan's log repo (GitHub issues of temporalio/dandavison-log; local clone
~/src/temporalio-etc/log). What they know means knowledge, understanding, beliefs, decisions
and open questions, not a summary of what was said.

Do not ask Dan anything before the report. Dan's words (the arguments)
give the topic, and may name sessions, and may name a target issue.

# Steps

1. Run `agent sessions sync`. Find the sessions on the topic with `agent sessions search`
   (several queries) and `agent sessions show ID --turns`, plus any Dan named. Skip sessions
   with nothing of substance on the topic, and say why in the report.
2. Choose the issues:
   - Without a target issue: the log issues these sessions have posted to on the topic. Find
     them in their transcripts (`agent sessions cat ID --tools --whole`, grep for
     `gh issue comment` and `issuecomment-`). If there are none, open a new issue with a terse
     description of the topic as its body, and use that one.
   - With a target issue: that issue only, whatever the sessions posted to before. If Dan asked
     for a new issue, open it.
3. Fetch each issue in full with `gh-issue-fetch N`.
4. Ask each session, one at a time, whether it knows anything valuable on the topic that the
   issues do not already contain, and if so to post it as a new comment on the right issue.
   Give it the issues' content inline in the prompt, not as file references, so that it reads
   them in full. Re-fetch the issues before each session, so that later sessions see what
   earlier ones posted.
5. If you opened an issue, read all its comments at the end and replace its body with a terse
   summary of them.

# Asking a session

Post the prompt as a comment on the session's control issue (`agent sessions issue ID` creates
it and prints it) and run `agent sessions attend --once`, which runs the turn headless and posts
the answer there.

- Never ask a session that is open in a terminal: attend would stop that process and close
  Dan's pane. A session is open if a file in ~/.claude/sessions/*.json has its id as
  `sessionId` and its `pid` is alive (`ps -p PID`). List such sessions in the report, with the
  prompt for Dan to paste into them himself.
- A GitHub comment holds at most 65,536 characters. When the inline issues are longer, split
  the prompt into numbered parts, one comment each, and say in every part but the last: reply
  only "received". Each part is a separate turn.
- Do not push a session into compaction: its context plus the inline material must stay well
  below its model's context window (`agent sessions show ID` gives its context size).
- Attend refuses prompts older than ten minutes, and has hourly and daily spending limits. If
  it refuses, or `~/.agent-sessions/tripped` exists, stop and report; never clear the breaker.

# Report

End with a short report for Dan:

- The issues, with links, and any you opened.
- Each session asked: whether it had anything new, and the links to the comments it posted.
- Sessions skipped, and why.
- Sessions open in a terminal, each with the prompt for Dan to paste.
